  "wikipedia": {
    "title": "Upsilon2 Cassiopeiae",
    "description": "Star in the constellation Cassiopeia",
    "summary": "Upsilon2 Cassiopeiae is a solitary star in the constellation of Cassiopeia, a few degrees to the south of Gamma Cassiopeiae. It has the proper name Castula, which has been officially adopted by the IAU. The star has a yellow hue and is faintly visible to the naked eye with an apparent visual magnitude of +4.62. Based upon parallax measurements, it is located at a distance of approximately 189 light years from the Sun. The star is drifting closer with a radial velocity of −48 km/s.",
    "intro": "Upsilon2 Cassiopeiae is a solitary star in the constellation of Cassiopeia, a few degrees to the south of Gamma Cassiopeiae. It has the proper name Castula , which has been officially adopted by the IAU. The star has a yellow hue and is faintly visible to the naked eye with an apparent visual magnitude of +4.62. Based upon parallax measurements, it is located at a distance of approximately 189 light years from the Sun. The star is drifting closer with a radial velocity of −48 km/s.\n\nThis is an aging giant star with a stellar classification of G8 IIIb Fe−0.5, where the suffix notation indicates a mild underabundance of iron in the spectrum. With the supply of core hydrogen exhausted, this star has cooled and expanded off the main sequence – at present it has 10 times the girth of the Sun. It is a red clump giant, which means it is on the horizontal branch undergoing core helium fusion.\nUpsilon2 Cassiopeiae is a barium star, showing an excess of the element barium in its spectrum. This can occur from mass transfer from a more-evolved companion star that later became a white dwarf, although no companion has been detected. It is 2.2 billion years old with 1.44 times the mass of the Sun. The star is radiating 55 times the luminosity of the Sun from its enlarged photosphere at an effective temperature of 4,937 K.",
    "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upsilon2_Cassiopeiae",
    "thumbnail": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Cassiopeia_constellation_map.svg/330px-Cassiopeia_constellation_map.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
